Subject: [PATCH] qemu: connect the instance's netstack through the dgram netdev

startUsernet gives QEMU its own gvisor-tap-vsock netstack through the
`socket` netdev (a length-prefixed stream on a unix socket). That netdev,
like `stream`, aborts QEMU in this case: net_socket_send() and
net_stream_data_send() ignore a recv() that fails with EWOULDBLOCK, but
then fall through to net_fill_rstate(rs, buf, size) with size == -1,
which skips its loop and fails `assert(size == 0)`:

    qemu-system-x86_64: ../net/net.c:2172: net_fill_rstate: Assertion
    `size == 0' failed.

QEMU exits with SIGABRT, the hostagent stops the instance, and the guest
loses its network in the middle of what it was doing (an apt install, in
agent-vm). It happens on Linux hosts with KVM, under sustained traffic,
with QEMU 10.2.x, to everyone whose guest goes through that netstack
(Lima's user-v2 network, Colima: qemu-project/qemu#3285,
estuary/flow#3267). What makes recv() return EAGAIN after poll() said
readable is not known. The fault is QEMU's: a unix stream socket whose
only pending data is an out-of-band byte does the same, with no netstack
(`-netdev socket,fd=N` or `-netdev stream`, the peer sends one MSG_OOB
byte).

The dgram netdev (QEMU 7.2 or later) returns on any failed recv() and has
no framing to lose. With it, the netstack is served on a datagram
socketpair, as vz does for its network (createSockPair, AcceptBess): one
end is passed to QEMU (-netdev dgram,local.type=fd), the other is
served by usernet.GVisorNetstackOpts.DgramConn. A unix datagram socket
keeps frame boundaries and loses nothing: a full buffer blocks the
sender, and QEMU stops reading until it can write.

Which one QEMU gets is decided by Cmdline, from the netdevs it lists, and
the netstack starts after it: it listens on the unix socket only for the
socket netdev, of a QEMU without dgram. Windows has no unix datagram
sockets and no file descriptor passing: it keeps the stream netdev, so the
abort remains possible there.

Tests: TestDgramConn (a frame sent as a datagram is answered by the
netstack; it times out without DgramConn being served), TestDgramPair,
TestFdDgramTemplate. The netdev choice in Cmdline needs a QEMU binary:
checked by agent-vm's e2e (internal/vm/lima_e2e_test.go), whose guest
boots and installs packages through it.

